    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d356-a13">

                <pb facs="0634" n="506" sameAs="frus:frus1885/pg506"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure 13 in No.
                    723.—Translation.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. St. Victor to Mr. Langston</hi>.</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State of Foreign Relations</hi>,<lb/><hi
                                rendition="#italic">Port-au-Prince</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1885-03-06"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >March</hi> 6, 1885</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>Mr. <hi rendition="#small-caps">Minister</hi>: I have the honor to inform you
                    that I have lately seen Mr. Weymann, whom I have asked to explain to you the
                    meaning of the paragraph of my dispatch of the 25th of February, presented in
                    your letter of the 2d March.</p>
                <p>Mr. Weymann has declared to me that he has given effect to my prayer, and that
                    to-day everything is understood between us on the subject of the basis to be
                    adopted for the settlement of the American reclamations connected with events of
                    September, 1883.</p>
                <p>I pray you, therefore, Mr. Minister, to be good enough to confirm to me this
                    understanding upon all the points of the question, and upon the instructions to
                    be given to the mixed commission, as I have reiterated to you, the request to do
                    so in my dispatch of the 28th February last.</p>
                <p>You will accept, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>B. ST. VICTOR.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
